# =============================================================================
# Spark defaults — REFERENCE COPY, not the file the containers read
# =============================================================================
# The real `spark-defaults.conf` next to this file is rendered per deployment
# by `service_env._render_spark` and is gitignored, because it carries object
# storage credentials. This template exists so the shape is reviewable in the
# repository and so a reader can see what the rendered file contains without
# having a deployment in front of them. Same split as
# `stacks/litellm/config.yaml.template`.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL. Until Spark 4.2.0 this stack configured S3
# through `SPARK_HADOOP_fs_s3a_*` environment variables in the compose file.
# Those never did anything:
#
#   $ docker run --rm --entrypoint sh apache/spark:4.2.0 \
#       -c 'grep -c SPARK_HADOOP /opt/entrypoint.sh'
#   0
#
# The `SPARK_HADOOP_*` → config translation is a Bitnami-image feature. The
# official apache/spark image does not do it, and Spark itself reads only
# AWS_ENDPOINT_URL / AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY /
# AWS_SESSION_TOKEN from the environment. The variables were set on all three
# containers and silently ignored on all three. S3 access from Jupyter worked
# only because `stacks/jupyter/spark-init.py` reads the same variables with
# `os.environ.get()` and applies them by hand.
#
# A config file is also the only option that reaches every process that
# matters. Spark Connect does not propagate driver-side SparkConf — a client
# setting a catalog property gets ignored — so the configuration has to exist
# server-side. `--conf` flags in the compose `command:` would cover only
# `spark-connect`, leaving the master, the workers and any interactive
# `spark-shell` unconfigured.
# =============================================================================

# --- Object storage ----------------------------------------------------------
# Per-bucket, not global. This deployment can reach two stores with different
# endpoints and different credentials, and `fs.s3a.bucket.<name>.*` is the
# mechanism Hadoop provides for exactly that. A global `fs.s3a.endpoint` would
# force one of them to be wrong.
#
# `endpoint.region` is required, not decorative. Measured against a live
# deployment: writing to R2 without one fails with
#
#   AWSBadRequestException ... Status Code: 400, Request ID: null
#
# — a bare 400 with a null request id, because R2 rejects the request before
# parsing it. Hetzner happens to tolerate the omission, which is precisely why
# both are set: one store working proves nothing about the other.
spark.hadoop.fs.s3a.impl                             org.apache.hadoop.fs.s3a.S3AFileSystem
spark.hadoop.fs.s3a.path.style.access                true

# Cloudflare R2 — the data lake. Survives a rebuild teardown, which the
# server's own disk does not.
spark.hadoop.fs.s3a.bucket.<R2_BUCKET>.endpoint          <R2_ENDPOINT>
spark.hadoop.fs.s3a.bucket.<R2_BUCKET>.endpoint.region   auto
spark.hadoop.fs.s3a.bucket.<R2_BUCKET>.access.key        <R2_ACCESS_KEY>
spark.hadoop.fs.s3a.bucket.<R2_BUCKET>.secret.key        <R2_SECRET_KEY>

# Hetzner Object Storage — kept because existing notebooks and the Jupyter
# stack address it, and removing it would break them for no gain.
spark.hadoop.fs.s3a.bucket.<HETZNER_BUCKET>.endpoint          <HETZNER_ENDPOINT>
spark.hadoop.fs.s3a.bucket.<HETZNER_BUCKET>.endpoint.region   <first label of the host, e.g. fsn1>
spark.hadoop.fs.s3a.bucket.<HETZNER_BUCKET>.access.key        <HETZNER_ACCESS_KEY>
spark.hadoop.fs.s3a.bucket.<HETZNER_BUCKET>.secret.key        <HETZNER_SECRET_KEY>

# --- Redaction ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Spark's default is `(?i)secret|password|token`, which redacts
# `fs.s3a.secret.key` and leaves `fs.s3a.access.key` in plain sight. The master
# UI on port 8088 renders the environment page for every running application,
# so the default is not enough here. Widened to cover access keys too.
spark.redaction.regex   (?i)secret|password|token|access[._]?key|credential
